WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.370
- Is Discord trying to screw
you out of a class-action?

00:00:03.370 --> 00:00:05.870
Is BetterHelp offering fake therapy?

00:00:05.870 --> 00:00:06.983
Let's find out.

00:00:06.983 --> 00:00:09.650
(chiming music)

00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:14.960
Hey LegalEagles, it's time to
litigate the terms of service

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:16.800
for Discord and BetterHelp.

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:18.970
If you have a suggestion
for the next legal topic,

00:00:18.970 --> 00:00:21.370
please leave it below, and
if you disagree with me,

00:00:21.370 --> 00:00:22.550
please be sure to leave your comment

00:00:22.550 --> 00:00:24.710
in the form of an objection.

00:00:24.710 --> 00:00:26.290
But remember to keep things civil,

00:00:26.290 --> 00:00:28.190
because Stella, the Legal Beagle,

00:00:28.190 --> 00:00:29.550
carries the gavel of justice,

00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:32.310
and she's not afraid to use
it in the comments section.

00:00:32.310 --> 00:00:34.430
Alright, let's think like a lawyer.

00:00:34.430 --> 00:00:38.540
Today we're talking
TOS's, terms of service.

00:00:38.540 --> 00:00:41.960
These are usually really
dry, boring contracts,

00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:44.910
but there are two particular
TOS's in the news recently,

00:00:44.910 --> 00:00:48.790
those from Discord and
BetterHelp, and not in a good way.

00:00:48.790 --> 00:00:50.910
There are two issues that
I wanna discuss today.

00:00:50.910 --> 00:00:53.860
Number one, is Discord's
arbitration clause

00:00:53.860 --> 00:00:56.270
and class-action waiver legal?

00:00:56.270 --> 00:00:59.020
And number two, does BetterHelp's terms

00:00:59.020 --> 00:01:02.040
mean that they are offering sham services?

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:04.840
What is a TOS, or term of service?

00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:07.840
A term of service is that
long contract that you sign

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:10.880
when you sign up for a
website, or some software,

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:12.520
that governs your relationship.

00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:16.720
And I'm sure, everyone here
has read the entire contract

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:17.870
before clicking accept,

00:01:17.870 --> 00:01:20.430
and then going on to use a website, right?

00:01:20.430 --> 00:01:23.210
Right, no one reads all
of the terms of service,

00:01:23.210 --> 00:01:25.260
but despite the fact
that you may not read it,

00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:27.740
the term of service still
governs the relationship

00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:29.340
between you and that website,

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:31.390
or the software that you're using.

00:01:31.390 --> 00:01:33.410
The term of service will
do a couple of things.

00:01:33.410 --> 00:01:36.960
Number one, they set out the
rules by which you must abide

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:39.050
if you are going to continue to use

00:01:39.050 --> 00:01:40.890
their software or service.

00:01:40.890 --> 00:01:43.050
And number two, they also provide

00:01:43.050 --> 00:01:46.710
the obligations that the
company is going to give to you

00:01:46.710 --> 00:01:51.710
in return for you being a user
of that website or software.

00:01:51.740 --> 00:01:53.270
But a term of service doesn't have

00:01:53.270 --> 00:01:55.140
to have any particular terms.

00:01:55.140 --> 00:01:56.310
It can lay out rules,

00:01:56.310 --> 00:01:58.530
it can create obligations
on behalf of the website,

00:01:58.530 --> 00:02:00.070
it can create obligations on behalf

00:02:00.070 --> 00:02:02.000
of the user to the website.

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:04.280
Or, it can be simply a disclaimer,

00:02:04.280 --> 00:02:06.440
that disclaims any kind of warranty,

00:02:06.440 --> 00:02:08.440
or any kind of relationship at all

00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:12.170
between the website itself,
and the users of that website,

00:02:12.170 --> 00:02:13.900
to try and get rid of liability

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:16.650
as between the users
and the website itself.

00:02:16.650 --> 00:02:20.050
Today we're going to talk
about two TOS's in particular,

00:02:20.050 --> 00:02:21.750
those from a company called Discord,

00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:24.230
which provides VOIP, or VoIP services,

00:02:24.230 --> 00:02:26.490
and the second from a
company called BetterHelp,

00:02:26.490 --> 00:02:31.130
which provides online teletherapy
services to its users.

00:02:31.130 --> 00:02:33.940
The first TOS I wanna
discuss comes from Discord.

00:02:33.940 --> 00:02:36.560
What's controversial here
is that a few days ago,

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:40.070
Discord created a new term in their TOS

00:02:40.070 --> 00:02:41.740
that is a class-action waiver.

00:02:41.740 --> 00:02:45.780
Ostensibly, it prevents any
user of the Discord services

00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:49.610
from filing a class-action
lawsuit, and instead requires

00:02:49.610 --> 00:02:53.230
those users to go into what's
called binding arbitration,

00:02:53.230 --> 00:02:55.370
instead of using the courts.

00:02:55.370 --> 00:02:56.990
So let's take a step back first,

00:02:56.990 --> 00:02:59.940
and talk about what is a class-action?

00:02:59.940 --> 00:03:03.060
A class action is a way of aggregating

00:03:03.060 --> 00:03:07.170
harms that are so small, that
no one would ever file suit,

00:03:07.170 --> 00:03:09.080
as a result of that particular harm.

00:03:09.080 --> 00:03:11.320
There was a movement that
started in the states,

00:03:11.320 --> 00:03:14.420
and then was governed
mostly by federal law,

00:03:14.420 --> 00:03:17.540
which allows a bunch of
really small claimants

00:03:17.540 --> 00:03:19.560
to aggregate their claims together.

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:22.420
Now, there are a ton of requirements.

00:03:22.420 --> 00:03:25.130
Rule 23 of the Federal
Rules of Civil Procedure

00:03:25.130 --> 00:03:28.250
requires that you have to have
things like an adequate class

00:03:28.250 --> 00:03:30.150
that must be ascertainable.

00:03:30.150 --> 00:03:32.310
There must be enough of those claims

00:03:32.310 --> 00:03:34.180
to make it basically worthwhile,

00:03:34.180 --> 00:03:36.460
to file a class-action suit.

00:03:36.460 --> 00:03:39.670
Those claimants must have a common claim,

00:03:39.670 --> 00:03:41.500
and that the class representative

00:03:41.500 --> 00:03:43.000
must have a claim that's typical,

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:45.320
of those common claims of the class.

00:03:45.320 --> 00:03:46.650
And there are other requirements,

00:03:46.650 --> 00:03:49.010
like the representative counsel

00:03:49.010 --> 00:03:51.120
must be adequate and a
number of other things.

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:53.960
But the bottom line is that
a class-action lawsuit is

00:03:53.960 --> 00:03:58.960
a way for one, or a
handful of class members

00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:01.600
to basically put forward a case

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:05.120
on behalf of the entire class of people.

00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:08.560
So instead of just filing
suit on behalf of yourself,

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:10.380
as you would in a normal lawsuit,

00:04:10.380 --> 00:04:12.930
you're filing suit with
the help of your attorneys

00:04:12.930 --> 00:04:16.330
on behalf of hundreds
potentially thousands,

00:04:16.330 --> 00:04:18.570
and sometimes even millions of people.

00:04:18.570 --> 00:04:21.960
And by aggregating all of
those small things together,

00:04:21.960 --> 00:04:23.720
you create a financial incentive

00:04:23.720 --> 00:04:25.800
to go forward with that case.

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:27.810
A class action lawsuit historically

00:04:27.810 --> 00:04:29.710
has been done by the attorney general,

00:04:29.710 --> 00:04:33.080
but now for the most part it's
done by private individuals.

00:04:33.080 --> 00:04:35.740
So you might ask yourself,
why would someone file

00:04:35.740 --> 00:04:37.320
a class-action lawsuit?

00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:39.670
Well, it's partially
the people themselves,

00:04:39.670 --> 00:04:41.090
and it's partially the attorneys.

00:04:41.090 --> 00:04:44.240
The attorneys are gonna get
a large cut of the payout.

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:46.910
Often you are aggregating so many

00:04:46.910 --> 00:04:48.700
of these small claims together,

00:04:48.700 --> 00:04:52.330
that a class-action lawsuit is
often a bet the company case.

00:04:52.330 --> 00:04:53.770
So that if the company lost it,

00:04:53.770 --> 00:04:56.090
and had to pay out what
the plaintiff is claiming,

00:04:56.090 --> 00:04:58.290
it might bankrupt the entire company.

00:04:58.290 --> 00:05:00.760
I've handled a ton of
class-actions before,

00:05:00.760 --> 00:05:03.260
usually defending very innocent companies

00:05:03.260 --> 00:05:05.170
from scurrilous plaintiff attorneys

00:05:05.170 --> 00:05:06.520
just trying to make a buck.

00:05:06.520 --> 00:05:09.200
But, you know, I've also been
a member of a class before,

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:11.470
and I've gotten one of
those two dollar checks

00:05:11.470 --> 00:05:13.910
from being a class member in a very

00:05:13.910 --> 00:05:15.710
small class-action recovery.

00:05:15.710 --> 00:05:17.280
So, I see it both ways.

00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:20.420
I understand the need
for companies to have

00:05:20.420 --> 00:05:22.240
a punitive action taken against them,

00:05:22.240 --> 00:05:24.180
when they've clearly done something wrong,

00:05:24.180 --> 00:05:26.730
but I've also seen plaintiff's
attorneys out there

00:05:26.730 --> 00:05:28.530
just in it to get the attorney's fees

00:05:28.530 --> 00:05:30.220
from that big pool of money.

00:05:30.220 --> 00:05:32.970
So Discord changed its term of service,

00:05:32.970 --> 00:05:35.900
and now requires a class-action waiver,

00:05:35.900 --> 00:05:38.090
and a private arbitration clause.

00:05:38.090 --> 00:05:40.190
Which means instead of being able to use

00:05:40.190 --> 00:05:41.440
the regular court system,

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:44.550
and using a class-action in
the regular court system,

00:05:44.550 --> 00:05:47.210
they force you into private arbitration,

00:05:47.210 --> 00:05:50.280
where you're not able to
file a class-action lawsuit.

00:05:50.280 --> 00:05:52.500
Now, arbitration sounds scary,

00:05:52.500 --> 00:05:56.670
but arbitration is really just
an informal version of court.

00:05:56.670 --> 00:05:58.620
The main difference is that generally,

00:05:58.620 --> 00:06:01.050
both parties have to hire the arbitrator,

00:06:01.050 --> 00:06:03.240
so instead of regular court
where you're not paying

00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:07.130
for the salary of the
judge, in arbitration,

00:06:07.130 --> 00:06:10.210
both sides usually have to pay
for the arbitrator's salary.

00:06:10.210 --> 00:06:13.720
So it's more informal, you can
set whatever rules you want,

00:06:13.720 --> 00:06:15.650
as long as they're agreed
upon by both sides,

00:06:15.650 --> 00:06:18.200
and you have to pay for
the arbitrators time.

00:06:18.200 --> 00:06:20.810
Now, Discord came out
with a statement defending

00:06:20.810 --> 00:06:23.130
their decision to include
these arbitration agreements,

00:06:23.130 --> 00:06:27.090
saying that class-actions can be very bad.

00:06:27.090 --> 00:06:29.290
They're used by attorneys
to get huge fees,

00:06:29.290 --> 00:06:31.990
and they take those fees
out of the class recovery,

00:06:31.990 --> 00:06:34.430
and that people often
get really small rewards

00:06:34.430 --> 00:06:35.300
they don't care about

00:06:35.300 --> 00:06:38.020
and they often just
throw away those checks.

00:06:38.020 --> 00:06:41.000
Now all of those things are in fact true,

00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:44.040
but you could see them
as features, not bugs.

00:06:44.040 --> 00:06:47.160
Again, often if people
weren't able to aggregate

00:06:47.160 --> 00:06:49.400
all these claims, they never
would have been incentivized

00:06:49.400 --> 00:06:51.220
to file the suit in the first place.

00:06:51.220 --> 00:06:54.180
And believe me, lawyers
don't like to work for free,

00:06:54.180 --> 00:06:57.320
so unless there is a big enough incentive,

00:06:57.320 --> 00:06:59.120
they won't handle those cases.

00:06:59.120 --> 00:07:01.650
I've handled both court
cases and arbitration,

00:07:01.650 --> 00:07:03.530
and I can't say for a fact that

00:07:03.530 --> 00:07:06.160
one is necessarily better than the other.

00:07:06.160 --> 00:07:08.060
Arbitration is almost certainly faster,

00:07:08.060 --> 00:07:10.040
because courts are really backed up.

00:07:10.040 --> 00:07:12.940
Arbitration gives you
individualized attention,

00:07:12.940 --> 00:07:16.680
where as in court the judge
is spreading his of her time,

00:07:16.680 --> 00:07:18.770
between hundreds or thousands

00:07:18.770 --> 00:07:21.020
of different cases at any given time,

00:07:21.020 --> 00:07:22.000
where as in arbitration,

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:24.540
you might get one to three retired judges

00:07:24.540 --> 00:07:28.210
who probably aren't
handling that many cases.

00:07:28.210 --> 00:07:30.770
Arbitration is generally
way more expensive

00:07:30.770 --> 00:07:32.620
because both parties have to pay

00:07:32.620 --> 00:07:34.180
the arbitrator's hourly.

00:07:34.180 --> 00:07:35.900
And I think the last arbitrator

00:07:35.900 --> 00:07:39.050
I had to hire was $900 an hour.

00:07:39.050 --> 00:07:41.080
They can charge these
fees because they have

00:07:41.080 --> 00:07:43.680
a certain pedigree from being ex-judges

00:07:43.680 --> 00:07:45.660
and really knowing what
they're talking about,

00:07:45.660 --> 00:07:49.390
and good arbitrators are
really important to hire.

00:07:49.390 --> 00:07:51.470
But I can't say that an
arbitration necessarily

00:07:51.470 --> 00:07:53.320
favors the defendant, which
is something that I see

00:07:53.320 --> 00:07:55.170
people saying all the time.

00:07:55.170 --> 00:07:58.110
I think people usually say
that because of the cost,

00:07:58.110 --> 00:07:59.680
but again, arbitrators themselves

00:07:59.680 --> 00:08:02.730
are just as good as regular judges,

00:08:02.730 --> 00:08:04.560
so I don't think that they are going to be

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:08.820
biased in favor of the
defendant, necessarily.

00:08:08.820 --> 00:08:10.110
Here in particular though,

00:08:10.110 --> 00:08:13.120
the fees issue isn't as bad
as it normally would be.

00:08:13.120 --> 00:08:16.760
In Discord's TOS they say
specifically that they will pay

00:08:16.760 --> 00:08:20.550
for any fees over what it
would cost to file in court,

00:08:20.550 --> 00:08:23.030
unless the arbitrator
finds that the case itself

00:08:23.030 --> 00:08:24.710
was filed frivolously.

00:08:24.710 --> 00:08:26.710
So what are the main
complaints that you might have

00:08:26.710 --> 00:08:29.310
about arbitration, in
the consumer context,

00:08:29.310 --> 00:08:31.920
where what consumer is gonna want to fight

00:08:31.920 --> 00:08:33.770
a hundred dollar case,

00:08:33.770 --> 00:08:34.860
if they're gonna have to pay out

00:08:34.860 --> 00:08:37.440
hundreds of dollars in
fees to the arbitrator.

00:08:37.440 --> 00:08:40.020
Well here, Discord is already agreeing

00:08:40.020 --> 00:08:42.310
to pay for the fees of the arbitrator.

00:08:42.310 --> 00:08:45.180
All you have to pay for
are the normal filing fees

00:08:45.180 --> 00:08:46.570
that it would cost to file suit.

00:08:46.570 --> 00:08:48.730
Now those themselves can be
in the hundreds of dollars,

00:08:48.730 --> 00:08:51.387
but still, that nothing
compared to paying an arbitrator

00:08:51.387 --> 00:08:55.090
$900 an hour for dozens,
if not hundreds of hours.

00:08:55.090 --> 00:08:57.300
Now interestingly, this
actually might give

00:08:57.300 --> 00:08:59.350
you more leverage against Discord

00:08:59.350 --> 00:09:01.620
because since Discord is on the hook

00:09:01.620 --> 00:09:04.120
to pay for these arbitrator services,

00:09:04.120 --> 00:09:07.250
Discord might rather just settle the case

00:09:07.250 --> 00:09:09.860
for a nominal fee instead
of having to go through

00:09:09.860 --> 00:09:11.610
expensive arbitration,
where they're paying

00:09:11.610 --> 00:09:13.930
for not only their own attorneys,

00:09:13.930 --> 00:09:16.750
but also the fees of
the arbitrator as well.

00:09:16.750 --> 00:09:18.580
So ironically, this term of service

00:09:18.580 --> 00:09:21.670
may actually give some
leverage to the consumer.

00:09:21.670 --> 00:09:22.610
But on the other hand,

00:09:22.610 --> 00:09:25.410
the TOS does clearly require you to waive

00:09:25.410 --> 00:09:27.000
your class-action rights,

00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:29.600
so you can't file a class-action lawsuit.

00:09:29.600 --> 00:09:31.330
Now, the big benefit as we discussed

00:09:31.330 --> 00:09:33.590
is being able to aggregate
all the small claims

00:09:33.590 --> 00:09:36.440
that no one would individually
want to file suit over.

00:09:36.440 --> 00:09:38.550
And instead, it requires you to file

00:09:38.550 --> 00:09:41.920
an individual suit on your own basis.

00:09:41.920 --> 00:09:44.750
So you lose the benefit
of having this litigation,

00:09:44.750 --> 00:09:46.310
if there is litigation taken care

00:09:46.310 --> 00:09:48.370
of for you by some other attorney

00:09:48.370 --> 00:09:50.280
and some other representative plaintiff.

00:09:50.280 --> 00:09:53.130
And frankly, it's a
relatively small chance

00:09:53.130 --> 00:09:54.520
that it's going to be relevant to you,

00:09:54.520 --> 00:09:55.810
and an even smaller chance

00:09:55.810 --> 00:09:57.930
that it's a recovery that you care about.

00:09:57.930 --> 00:10:00.820
But, it certainly does
prevent anyone from taking

00:10:00.820 --> 00:10:04.110
punitive action against Discord
for violation of the law,

00:10:04.110 --> 00:10:06.620
and some people think that lawsuits,

00:10:06.620 --> 00:10:08.740
and especially class-action lawsuits,

00:10:08.740 --> 00:10:12.150
are created specifically
for their punitive action

00:10:12.150 --> 00:10:14.460
to make sure companies stay in line,

00:10:14.460 --> 00:10:15.980
even when the recoveries are

00:10:15.980 --> 00:10:18.340
very very small on an individual basis.

00:10:18.340 --> 00:10:19.173
So at the end of the day,

00:10:19.173 --> 00:10:21.820
this is a mixed bag for
the users of Discord.

00:10:21.820 --> 00:10:23.750
It's not as scary as it first seems,

00:10:23.750 --> 00:10:25.540
but there's actually some
good things in there.

00:10:25.540 --> 00:10:27.340
It allows you to go through arbitration,

00:10:27.340 --> 00:10:29.170
which can be much faster than courts,

00:10:29.170 --> 00:10:32.500
and much more informal, on Discord's dime.

00:10:32.500 --> 00:10:34.240
However, you do give up the right

00:10:34.240 --> 00:10:35.810
to file class-action lawsuit,

00:10:35.810 --> 00:10:38.590
and you may lose out on some small checks

00:10:38.590 --> 00:10:40.850
that would have been done
by class-action attorneys

00:10:40.850 --> 00:10:44.010
and class-action plaintiffs,
in some other universe.

00:10:44.010 --> 00:10:45.270
So there are some good things,

00:10:45.270 --> 00:10:48.210
and some bad things in
this new Discord TOS.

00:10:48.210 --> 00:10:50.220
But the question is, is it legal?

00:10:50.220 --> 00:10:51.770
Well, recent supreme court

00:10:51.770 --> 00:10:54.680
does in fact allow these
class-action waivers.

00:10:54.680 --> 00:10:59.090
Starting in 2011, in the
AT&amp;T versus Concepcion case,

00:10:59.090 --> 00:11:02.550
and recently reaffirmed this
year in Epic versus Lewis,

00:11:02.550 --> 00:11:04.730
and Ernst and Young versus Morris,

00:11:04.730 --> 00:11:07.050
class-action waivers are enforceable,

00:11:07.050 --> 00:11:10.210
and companies can force you
into private arbitration.

00:11:10.210 --> 00:11:13.170
So you might not like it,
but it's probably legal.

00:11:13.170 --> 00:11:15.510
So let's move on to the
second term of service,

00:11:15.510 --> 00:11:17.750
that from the company called BetterHelp.

00:11:17.750 --> 00:11:19.270
My understanding is that BetterHelp

00:11:19.270 --> 00:11:22.070
is an online platform that links

00:11:22.070 --> 00:11:24.670
online therapists with consumers.

00:11:24.670 --> 00:11:27.150
I've never used BetterHelp,

00:11:27.150 --> 00:11:28.620
unlike a lot of people on YouTube,

00:11:28.620 --> 00:11:30.760
I am not sponsored by BetterHelp,

00:11:30.760 --> 00:11:33.360
but I can certainly see the benefit of

00:11:33.360 --> 00:11:37.340
being able to just dial up
a therapist using Skype,

00:11:37.340 --> 00:11:40.410
instead of having to
drive over to a therapist

00:11:40.410 --> 00:11:41.340
and see them in person.

00:11:41.340 --> 00:11:42.980
Maybe that's a good service, I don't know.

00:11:42.980 --> 00:11:45.920
But what I can tell you is that
there is a huge controversy

00:11:45.920 --> 00:11:49.040
brewing over BetterHelp's term of service.

00:11:49.040 --> 00:11:50.930
As of a few weeks ago,

00:11:50.930 --> 00:11:54.390
the BetterHelp TOS implied that
they didn't the credentials

00:11:54.390 --> 00:11:57.650
or the background of all the
therapeutic professionals

00:11:57.650 --> 00:11:59.430
that they use to link up

00:11:59.430 --> 00:12:02.340
between consumers and the therapist.

00:12:02.340 --> 00:12:04.530
So, let's look at this disclaimer

00:12:04.530 --> 00:12:06.640
in the original terms of service.

00:12:06.640 --> 00:12:09.130
It seems pretty clear that the old TOS

00:12:09.130 --> 00:12:12.710
says that BetterHelp was
not obligated to check out

00:12:12.710 --> 00:12:15.010
the background or the credentials

00:12:15.010 --> 00:12:17.510
of the therapists that they use.

00:12:17.510 --> 00:12:19.780
Now, even as originally written,

00:12:19.780 --> 00:12:22.980
this is a floor, this is
a disclaimer of liability,

00:12:22.980 --> 00:12:25.250
it's a statement that
there is no obligation,

00:12:25.250 --> 00:12:28.700
but it's not a statement that
BetterHelp was absolutely not

00:12:28.700 --> 00:12:31.300
checking on their professionals.

00:12:31.300 --> 00:12:33.410
According to BetterHelp themselves,

00:12:33.410 --> 00:12:35.690
they were checking on the backgrounds

00:12:35.690 --> 00:12:37.220
and credentials of their therapists,

00:12:37.220 --> 00:12:39.240
but they didn't want to be on the hook

00:12:39.240 --> 00:12:41.800
for misstatements by those therapists,

00:12:41.800 --> 00:12:44.150
and they didn't want to be
obligated to undertake that

00:12:44.150 --> 00:12:47.380
even though they were generally
providing that service.

00:12:47.380 --> 00:12:49.620
Phil DeFranco did an
entire video about this,

00:12:49.620 --> 00:12:51.440
I'll link to that down below,

00:12:51.440 --> 00:12:52.640
and from what I can piece together,

00:12:52.640 --> 00:12:54.720
he seems to get things more or less right.

00:12:54.720 --> 00:12:56.780
- From that terms of service,
there's this question

00:12:56.780 --> 00:12:58.940
that pops up, okay, are you just allowing

00:12:58.940 --> 00:13:00.940
anyone to say that they're a counselor?

00:13:00.940 --> 00:13:02.310
Because that language is concerning

00:13:02.310 --> 00:13:04.730
and is completely alien
to my understanding

00:13:04.730 --> 00:13:06.470
of how they go and vet these people.

00:13:06.470 --> 00:13:08.740
From here I then reached out
to our contact at BetterHelp

00:13:08.740 --> 00:13:11.340
we ended up being connected
with the founder of BetterHelp,

00:13:11.340 --> 00:13:12.880
Alon Matas, and he gave me a statement

00:13:12.880 --> 00:13:14.900
where at one point he talked
about the terms and conditions

00:13:14.900 --> 00:13:16.990
and questions, saying The
Terms and Conditions document

00:13:16.990 --> 00:13:18.500
typically provides the legalese

00:13:18.500 --> 00:13:20.210
that defines the extent of liability

00:13:20.210 --> 00:13:22.930
and mitigates legal risks
associated with such platforms.

00:13:22.930 --> 00:13:25.200
We will be revising the
Terms and Conditions document

00:13:25.200 --> 00:13:26.980
so it more closely matches
the vetting process

00:13:26.980 --> 00:13:27.950
we currently conduct.

00:13:27.950 --> 00:13:29.680
- This is kind of standard legalese

00:13:29.680 --> 00:13:31.310
that you'll see in a TOS like this.

00:13:31.310 --> 00:13:33.500
It's about preventing legal liability,

00:13:33.500 --> 00:13:36.770
it is not a statement that BetterHelp was

00:13:36.770 --> 00:13:39.260
actively turning a blind
eye to their therapists.

00:13:39.260 --> 00:13:40.550
And my understanding is that

00:13:40.550 --> 00:13:43.067
in a reaction to this controversy,

00:13:43.067 --> 00:13:44.880
BetterHelp has revised
their term of service

00:13:44.880 --> 00:13:47.360
to more accurately reflect
what they actually do,

00:13:47.360 --> 00:13:49.910
and now they are taking on
the affirmative legal burden

00:13:49.910 --> 00:13:52.130
associated with those actions.

00:13:52.130 --> 00:13:53.480
But even as originally written,

00:13:53.480 --> 00:13:56.970
it doesn't seem like this was
much to do about anything.

00:13:56.970 --> 00:13:59.230
Additionally, PewDiePie,
who seems to have an

00:13:59.230 --> 00:14:01.700
ax to grind in this
particular controversy,

00:14:01.700 --> 00:14:05.240
was aggrieved by BetterHelp's
disclaimer of warranty,

00:14:05.240 --> 00:14:07.970
pointing out that BetterHelp stated that

00:14:07.970 --> 00:14:10.400
they don't stand by their professionals.

00:14:10.400 --> 00:14:12.630
Now this is called a disclaimer.

00:14:12.630 --> 00:14:14.810
This is very, very common.

00:14:14.810 --> 00:14:17.170
In fact, I would bet that in the TOS

00:14:17.170 --> 00:14:19.670
of almost every website
you interact with there is

00:14:19.670 --> 00:14:23.560
a disclaimer, that says
they disclaim any warranty

00:14:23.560 --> 00:14:25.630
for the services that they provide.

00:14:25.630 --> 00:14:27.291
And really, it's not surprising that

00:14:27.291 --> 00:14:29.110
BetterHelp would be any different.

00:14:29.110 --> 00:14:30.340
BetterHelp is basically just a platform

00:14:30.340 --> 00:14:32.930
that links up online
therapists to patients,

00:14:32.930 --> 00:14:35.360
so it's not surprising that the platform

00:14:35.360 --> 00:14:36.950
would want to disclaim liability

00:14:36.950 --> 00:14:40.230
from the independent contractors
that it contracts with.

00:14:40.230 --> 00:14:42.820
This is pretty standard
for any kind of platform,

00:14:42.820 --> 00:14:45.240
and almost any website
that you do business with.

00:14:45.240 --> 00:14:48.250
For example, my guess is
that if you hire a plumber

00:14:48.250 --> 00:14:51.510
through Angie's List, Angie's
List is not going to guarantee

00:14:51.510 --> 00:14:53.290
the work of that plumber.

00:14:53.290 --> 00:14:55.870
This kind of disclaimer
is very very common,

00:14:55.870 --> 00:14:58.120
and it's certainly nothing
to freak out about.

00:14:58.120 --> 00:14:59.430
Similar terms are probably behind

00:14:59.430 --> 00:15:00.660
every website that you visit,

00:15:00.660 --> 00:15:02.290
and govern every relationship

00:15:02.290 --> 00:15:04.240
with any platform that you use.

00:15:04.240 --> 00:15:06.080
So I can't say whether you
should go to BetterHelp

00:15:06.080 --> 00:15:07.550
for therapy services,

00:15:07.550 --> 00:15:08.960
but I can say you should

00:15:08.960 --> 00:15:12.400
not go to PewDiePie for legal advice.

00:15:12.400 --> 00:15:13.233
Do you agree?

00:15:13.233 --> 00:15:14.730
Leave your objections in the comments,

00:15:14.730 --> 00:15:17.963
and until next time,
I'll see you in court.

